  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nTwo disturbances are clearly evident on water vapor imagery this \nafternoon. The first is the upper-level closed low spiraling around \nthe NE/KS/IA/MO borders whereas the second wave's axis plunges \nsouthward toward the Gulf. Phasing became increasingly obvious late \nthis morning as these two features approached one another with the \ndeepening of the closed low and the negative tilt of the entire \nsystem. Gulf moisture continues to funnel into the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley, fueling widespread precipitation across southeastern \nMissouri, and southern Illinois. As of 1 PM, this precipitation has \nremained as rain with temperatures still comfortably above freezing. \nA precipitation type changeover is expected in these areas sometime \nlater this afternoon/evening. Thankfully, precipitation type \nchangeovers do not appear to be a concern for our forecast area with \nthe complete vertical profile expected to be subfreezing at the \nonset of precipitation. \n\nAs moisture wraps around the closed low, snow chances will begin to \ncreep into our northern and eastern tiers of counties after sunset, \nthough the greatest chances for snow (70-90%) are not expected to \narrive until after midnight. In these areas, the question is not \n\"will it snow?\" but \"will it accumulate?\". Accumulating snowfall \npotential is greatest overnight and before sunrise Thursday morning. \nHowever, even overnight, surface temperatures remain marginal and \nsnowfall rates generally low. Snow will have the greatest success \naccumulating on grassy and elevated surfaces but may struggle on \nwarmer surfaces not as susceptible to radiative cooling. This could \nlead to somewhat stark variations in snow totals, but even so, \naround 1 to 3 inches of snow is still the expectation for this snow \nevent. A Winter Weather Advisory will take effect late Wednesday \nevening for counties in central and northern Missouri along and east \nof US-65. \n\nWest of US-65 and the aforementioned Winter Weather Advisory, \nsnowfall accumulations will gradually taper off with a dusting to \n0.5\" or so of snow possible between US-65 and the I-35/I-49 \ncorridors. Elsewhere, it may be difficult to see much of any \nsnowfall accumulation with wrap-around moisture not arriving until \nearly/mid-morning when surface temperatures are already beginning to \nwarm above freezing. For this reason, very little accumulating \nsnowfall is expected for locations west of the I-35/I-49 corridors. \nFlurries remain possible in the early morning hours so long as the \nabove-surface layer can remain cold enough; if any of these flakes \nmanage to accumulate, they will likely quickly succumb to warming \ntemperatures. Precipitation chances will steadily decline after \nsunrise as the closed low slides to the southeast, reducing both \nlift and moisture. Some light flurries and/or sprinkles will remain \npossible through the afternoon hours, particularly across central \nMissouri. \n\nAfter this system clears out of the area, quasi-zonal flow should \nsettle in to close out the week and start the weekend. Broad WAA \nwill push temperatures back into the 50s by Saturday. Late Saturday \nnight, a wave embedded in the mean flow is progged to pass through \nthe area. The global suite seems to be growing more aligned on rain \npotential with NBM PoPs jumping about 20% (now a 60-80% chance) from \nthe previous forecast cycle, likely latching on to the greater \nconsensus just noted. A couple of tenths of rain seem possible, but \nguidance continues to deviate on rain totals. Temperatures on Sunday \nwill be slightly cooler behind the front but should rebound yet \nagain early next week.",
